# "motion" in S2 is motion evident to the senses, while S4 defines it as any reduction from potentiality to actuality. Sensible motion is a case of that broader change, so "some things are in motion" is true on either reading; I did not count it as two senses.
# "first mover" in S12 and S13 means the primary mover on which the others depend, not the earliest in time, and every statement that uses it reads it that way.
